[comment {To render: dtplite -o tlschan.n nroff tlschan.man
                    dtplite -o tlschan.html html tlschan.man
}]
[manpage_begin tlschan n 0.1]
[keywords TLS SSL socket channel certificate X.509 https ALPN wolfSSL rustls "Mbed TLS" OpenSSL]
[moddesc {TLS channels for Tcl 9}]
[titledesc {TLS 1.2 and 1.3 channels on wolfSSL, rustls, Mbed TLS or OpenSSL}]
[category Networking]
[require Tcl 9.0]
[require tlschan [opt 0.1]]
[require tlschan::wolfssl [opt 0.1]]
[require tlschan::rustls [opt 0.1]]
[require tlschan::mbedtls [opt 0.1]]
[require tlschan::openssl [opt 0.1]]
[description]

These packages add TLS to Tcl channels by stacking a TLS layer on a socket
or any other bidirectional channel. Four backend packages share one channel
layer and one command set and differ only in the TLS library underneath:

[list_begin definitions]
[def [package tlschan::wolfssl]] wolfSSL.
[def [package tlschan::rustls]] rustls with the ring crypto provider.
[def [package tlschan::mbedtls]] Mbed TLS 3.6.
[def [package tlschan::openssl]] OpenSSL 3.
[list_end]

Normally a program loads [package tlschan], which loads one backend and
makes its commands [cmd ::tlschan::][arg name]. This page writes the
commands that way. Each backend package also defines them in the namespace
of its own name, such as [namespace ::tlschan::wolfssl], with the same
syntax; requiring a backend directly pins it. Several backends can be loaded
into one interpreter, together with tcltls (package [package tls]).

[para]

[package tlschan] takes the backends in the order given by the variable
[var ::tlschan::prefer], set before loading it, else by the environment
variable [var TLSCHAN_BACKEND], else [const "openssl rustls mbedtls wolfssl"].
The first of them already loaded is chosen, else the first that loads. If
none does, [cmd "package require tlschan"] raises
[const "no TLS backend available (tried openssl, rustls, mbedtls, wolfssl)"]
with errorCode [const "TLS BACKEND"].

[example {
namespace eval tlschan {variable prefer {mbedtls openssl}}
package require tlschan
}]

[para]

Unless the command [cmd ::tls::socket] exists already, [package tlschan]
also creates aliases [cmd ::tls::][arg name] for the chosen backend's
commands, so that code written for tcltls runs unchanged. A tcltls loaded
afterwards replaces them. A backend package by itself never touches
[namespace ::tls]. See [sectref COMPATIBILITY] for differences from tcltls
and tclmtls.

[para]

Only TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 are implemented. By default a client opened with
[cmd tlschan::socket] verifies the server's certificate chain against the
system trust store and checks that the certificate matches the host name it
connected to.

[section COMMANDS]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd tlschan::socket] [opt [arg "option value ..."]] [arg host] [arg port]]

Opens a TCP connection with [cmd ::socket] and stacks TLS on it with
[cmd tlschan::import]. Returns the channel name. [option -async] (which
takes no value), [option -myaddr] and [option -myport] go to [cmd ::socket].
[option -autoservername] [arg bool], default [const 1], sets
[option -servername] to [arg host] when it is not given. All other options,
after the defaults set with [cmd tlschan::init], go to
[cmd tlschan::import]; see [sectref OPTIONS].

[para]

Errors from [cmd ::socket] (for example [const "couldn't open socket: connection refused"])
and from [cmd tlschan::import] are raised unchanged; when the import
fails the socket is closed. The handshake is not part of this command: it
runs on first use of the channel, see [sectref "CHANNEL BEHAVIOUR"]. With
[option -async], a failed connect is reported there, as in
[const "handshake failed: connection refused"].

[call [cmd tlschan::socket] [option -server] [arg command] [opt [arg "option value ..."]] [arg port]]

Opens a listening socket with [cmd "::socket -server"] and returns it.
[option -myaddr], [option -reuseaddr] and [option -reuseport] are passed to
[cmd ::socket]; [option -backlog] is not supported. Every accepted
connection is imported with [option -server] [const 1],
[option -model] set to the listening socket, and the other options, then
[arg command] is called with the channel, the client address and the client
port appended, as for [cmd "::socket -server"].

[para]

So all accepted connections share one configuration, and with it one
session ticket key (see [sectref "SESSION RESUMPTION"]): the first
connection reads the files and evaluates the [option -password] script, and
later ones reuse the result. An import error (an unreadable
[option -certfile], for example) closes that connection, is reported
through [cmd bgerror], and [arg command] is not called; the next connection
tries again. A [option -model] given here replaces the listening socket.

[call [cmd tlschan::import] [arg channel] [opt [arg "option value ..."]]]
[call [cmd tlschan::starttls] [arg channel] [opt [arg "option value ..."]]]

Stacks a TLS layer on [arg channel] and returns the channel name, which is
unchanged. [arg channel] can be any channel open for reading and writing.
No data is exchanged until the handshake starts, see
[sectref "CHANNEL BEHAVIOUR"]. The options are described in
[sectref OPTIONS]. [cmd starttls] is another name for [cmd import].

[para]

Options are checked and files loaded here: a bad option, file or password
raises an error from this command and leaves the channel as it was.

[call [cmd tlschan::unimport] [arg channel]]
[call [cmd tlschan::unstack] [arg channel]]

Sends a close_notify alert if the handshake has completed, writes out
pending TLS data and removes the TLS layer. The channel stays open and
carries plain data from then on. Raises
[const "bad channel \"chan\": not a TLS channel"] (errorCode
[const "TLS CHANNEL"]) if the topmost layer of [arg channel] is not TLS.
[cmd unstack] is another name for [cmd unimport].

[call [cmd tlschan::handshake] [arg channel]]

Advances the handshake. Returns [const 1] once the handshake has completed
and [const 0] if it is still in progress, which happens in nonblocking
mode and inside the channel's [option -validatecommand]; otherwise the
command waits for the handshake to finish.
If the handshake failed, raises its error message, such as
[const "handshake failed: certificate verify failed: certificate has expired"],
with errorCode [const "TLS HANDSHAKE"]. A
failed channel raises the same error on every later call.

[call [cmd tlschan::status] [opt [option -local]] [arg channel]]

Returns a dictionary describing the session and the peer's certificate, or
with [option -local] the local certificate. The dictionary is empty until
the handshake has completed, and stays empty if it failed. The certificate
keys are missing when there is no such certificate, for example on a server
whose client sent none. Session keys:

[list_begin definitions]
[def "[const protocol] [arg string]"]
[const TLSv1.2] or [const TLSv1.3], as in tcltls.
[def "[const cipher] [arg name]"]
IANA name of the cipher suite, such as [const TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384].
[def "[const sbits] [arg n]"]
Key size of the symmetric cipher in bits: [const 128] or [const 256].
[def "[const alpn] [arg protocol]"]
The protocol agreed through ALPN, or an empty string.
[def "[const servername] [arg name]"]
On a client the [option -servername] in use; on a server the name the
client sent in the SNI extension. Empty if there is none.
[list_end]

The certificate keys are the same as in the [arg cert] argument of the
[option -validatecommand] callback:

[list_begin definitions]
[def "[const certificate] [arg pem]"]
The certificate in PEM format.
[def "[const sha1_hash] [arg hex]"]
[def "[const sha256_hash] [arg hex]"]
SHA-1 and SHA-256 digest of the DER certificate, in upper case hex.
[def "[const subject] [arg dn]"]
[def "[const issuer] [arg dn]"]
Distinguished names in RFC 2253 form, most significant part last, as in
[const CN=localhost,O=Example,C=US]. Special characters are escaped with a
backslash; attributes of unknown type appear as [arg oid][const =#][arg hex].
[def "[const serialNumber] [arg hex]"]
[def "[const serial] [arg hex]"]
The serial number in upper case hex without leading zeros, preceded by
[const -] if negative. [const serial] is the tcltls 1.7 name.
[def "[const version] [arg n]"]
Certificate version, normally [const 3].
[def "[const signatureAlgorithm] [arg name]"]
[def "[const signature] [arg name]"]
Signature algorithm of the certificate (outer and inner field), such as
[const sha256WithRSAEncryption] or [const ecdsa-with-SHA256]; unknown
algorithms appear as dotted OIDs.
[def "[const notBefore] [arg time]"]
[def "[const notAfter] [arg time]"]
Validity period as [const "Oct 15 08:03:54 2026 GMT"]; the day of the
month is padded to two characters with a space.
[def "[const self_signed] [arg bool]"]
Whether issuer and subject are the same name.
[def "[const publicKeyAlgorithm] [arg name]"]
Such as [const rsaEncryption], [const id-ecPublicKey] or [const ED25519].
[def "[const bits] [arg n]"]
Size of the public key: the RSA modulus length, or the curve size for
elliptic curve keys ([const 256] for P-256). Missing if unknown.
[def "[const extensions] [arg list]"]
Names of the certificate's extensions in certificate order, dotted OIDs
for unknown ones.
[def "[const num_extensions] [arg n]"]
Length of that list.
[def "[const subjectAltName] [arg list]"]
Subject alternative names, each as [const DNS:][arg name],
[const "IP Address:"][arg address], [const email:][arg address],
[const URI:][arg uri], [const DirName:][arg /name] or
[const "Registered ID:"][arg oid]. IPv6 addresses are written as eight
colon separated hex groups. Missing without the extension.
[def "[const subjectKeyIdentifier] [arg hex]"]
[def "[const authorityKeyIdentifier] [arg hex]"]
Key identifiers in upper case hex. Missing without the extension.
[def "[const ocsp] [arg list]"]
OCSP responder URLs from the authority information access extension.
Missing if there are none.
[def "[const ca] [arg bool]"]
The CA flag of the basic constraints extension; [const 0] without it.
[list_end]

[call [cmd tlschan::connection] [arg channel]]

Returns a dictionary with the state of the connection, available at any
time:

[list_begin definitions]
[def "[const state] [arg state]"]
[const handshake], [const open] or [const failed].
[def "[const is_server] [arg bool]"]
Whether the channel is the server side.
[def "[const servername] [arg name]"]
As in [cmd status]; before the handshake has completed, the
[option -servername] given. Absent when the state is [const failed].
[def "[const error] [arg message]"]
Only when the state is [const failed]: the error message.
[def "[const protocol] [const cipher] [const sbits] [const alpn]"]
Only when the state is [const open]: as in [cmd status].
[def "[const session_reused] [arg bool]"]
Only when the state is [const open]: whether the session was resumed, see
[sectref "SESSION RESUMPTION"].
[list_end]

[call [cmd tlschan::init] [opt [arg "option value ..."]]]

Sets the default options of [cmd tlschan::socket], both forms. Each
call replaces the previous defaults; without arguments it removes them.
[option -model], [option -server] and [option -servername] are dropped. The
options are not checked until [cmd socket] uses them.
[cmd tlschan::import] does not use these defaults. Returns an empty
string.

[call [cmd tlschan::ciphers] [opt [arg protocol]] [opt [arg verbose]] [opt [arg supported]]]

Returns the IANA names of the cipher suites the library offers for
certificate based key exchange. [arg protocol] is one of [const ssl2],
[const ssl3], [const tls1], [const tls1.1], [const tls1.2] or
[const tls1.3]; the list is empty for the first four. Without
[arg protocol], suites of both TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 are returned.
[arg verbose] and [arg supported] are accepted for compatibility and
ignored.

[call [cmd tlschan::protocols]]

Returns the protocols that can be enabled: [const "tls1.2 tls1.3"].

[call [cmd tlschan::version]]

Returns the name and version of the TLS library, such as
[const "wolfSSL 5.9.4"], [const "rustls 0.23.46"],
[const "Mbed TLS 3.6.7"] or [const "OpenSSL 3.6.5"].

[call [cmd tlschan::exporter] [arg channel] [arg label] [arg length] [opt [arg context]]]

Returns [arg length] bytes of keying material exported from the session as
defined by RFC 5705 (TLS 1.2) and RFC 8446 section 7.5 (TLS 1.3).
[arg length] is 1 to 8160; [arg context] is a byte string. With TLS 1.2
leaving out [arg context] gives a different result from an empty one; with
TLS 1.3 the two are the same. Errors:
[const "the TLS handshake has not completed"] (errorCode
[const "TLS WRONGSTATE"]), [const "length must be between 1 and 8160"]
([const "TLS WRONGARGS"]) and [const "keying material export failed"]
([const "TLS EXPORT"]).

[call [cmd tlschan::randombytes] [arg count]]

Returns [arg count] bytes, 0 to 1048576, from the library's random number
generator.

[call [cmd tlschan::aesgcm-encrypt] [arg key] [arg plaintext]]

Encrypts [arg plaintext] with AES-256-GCM, without associated data.
[arg key] must be 32 bytes. Returns a fresh random 12 byte nonce, the
ciphertext and the 16 byte tag, concatenated: 28 bytes more than
[arg plaintext].

[call [cmd tlschan::aesgcm-decrypt] [arg key] [arg data]]

Reverses [cmd aesgcm-encrypt]: [arg data] is nonce, ciphertext and tag as
returned by it. Raises [const "ciphertext too short"] if [arg data] has
fewer than 28 bytes and [const "AES-GCM authentication failed"] if the tag
does not match.

[call [cmd tlschan::pbkdf2] [arg digest] [arg password] [arg salt] [arg iterations] [arg length]]

Derives [arg length] bytes, 1 to 64, from [arg password] and [arg salt]
with PBKDF2 (RFC 8018). [arg digest] is [const sha1], [const sha256] or
[const sha512], the HMAC hash. [arg iterations] is 1 to 10000000.

[para]

The arguments and results of [cmd randombytes], [cmd aesgcm-encrypt],
[cmd aesgcm-decrypt] and [cmd pbkdf2] are byte strings; a character above
U+00FF raises a Tcl error, so convert text with
[cmd "encoding convertto utf-8"] first. Their own errors have errorCode
[const "TLS CRYPTO"].

[call [cmd tlschan::pkgconfig] [method list]]
[call [cmd tlschan::pkgconfig] [method get] [arg key]]

Build information as registered with [fun Tcl_RegisterConfig]. Keys:
[const version], the package version ([const 0.1.0]), and [const backend],
one of [const wolfssl], [const rustls], [const mbedtls] or
[const openssl].

[call [cmd tlschan::backend]]

Only in [package tlschan]: returns the name of the backend it loaded, such
as [const openssl].

[list_end]

[section OPTIONS]

These options are accepted by [cmd import] and [cmd starttls], by
[cmd socket] and by [cmd init]. Boolean values are any form Tcl accepts. An
empty value counts as not given for [option -cadir], [option -cafile],
[option -cert], [option -certfile], [option -cipher],
[option -ciphersuites], [option -command], [option -key],
[option -keyfile], [option -model], [option -password],
[option -servername], [option -validatecommand] and [option -vcmd].

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -alpn [arg list]]
Protocol names to negotiate with ALPN (RFC 7301), such as
[const "h2 http/1.1"]. Each name must be 1 to 255 bytes long, or
[cmd import] raises [const "invalid ALPN protocol name \"\""] (errorCode
[const "TLS CONFIG"]). Which common name is chosen depends on the backend,
see [sectref "BACKEND DIFFERENCES"]. With none in common the handshake
completes without ALPN.

[opt_def -autoservername [arg bool]]
Only meaningful to [cmd socket], see there. [cmd import] ignores it.

[opt_def -cadir [arg directory]]
A directory of trusted CA certificates. The TLS library reads it directly,
so it must be a native path, not one inside a Tcl virtual filesystem. The
expected file names differ, see [sectref "BACKEND DIFFERENCES"].
[option -cadir] and [option -cafile] can be combined.

[opt_def -cafile [arg file]]
Trusted CA certificates: a PEM file with any number of them, or one DER
certificate. The file is read through Tcl's filesystem, so it can be inside
zipfs or another virtual filesystem.

[para]

Without [option -cafile] and [option -cadir] the system trust store is
used, read when first needed. Where it comes from depends on the backend,
see [sectref "BACKEND DIFFERENCES"].

[opt_def -cert [arg data]]
The local certificate as data: PEM text, which may hold the leaf certificate
followed by intermediates to be sent along, or a single DER certificate.
Takes precedence over [option -certfile].

[opt_def -certfile [arg file]]
As [option -cert], read from a file through Tcl's filesystem.

[opt_def -cipher [arg list]]
The TLS 1.2 cipher suites to enable, replacing the default ones. The list
holds suite names separated by colons, commas or white space: IANA names
as [cmd ciphers] returns them
([const TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256]), the same with
[const -] for [const _], OpenSSL names
([const ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256]) or the library's own names, in any
letter case. OpenSSL cipher string syntax ([const HIGH], [const !aNULL],
[const @STRENGTH]) is not supported. An unknown name raises
[const "unknown cipher: "][arg name] (errorCode [const "TLS CONFIG"]).

[opt_def -ciphersuites [arg list]]
The TLS 1.3 cipher suites to enable, replacing the default ones, in the
same form, such as [const TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256].

[para]

Names of either protocol may appear in either option: each name is sorted
to its protocol, and the names a protocol receives replace its defaults. A
protocol that receives no names keeps its defaults, unless its own option
was given: [option -cipher] without a TLS 1.2 name disables TLS 1.2, and
[option -ciphersuites] without a TLS 1.3 name disables TLS 1.3. If that
leaves no protocol, [cmd import] raises [const "no usable cipher suites"]
(errorCode [const "TLS CONFIG"]).

[opt_def -command [arg prefix]]
A command prefix invoked on handshake completion and on failure, see
[sectref CALLBACKS].

[opt_def -key [arg data]]
The private key as data, PEM or DER: PKCS#8, PKCS#1 (RSA) or SEC1 (EC).
Takes precedence over [option -keyfile]. A certificate and a key must be
given together, and they must match.

[opt_def -keyfile [arg file]]
As [option -key], read from a file through Tcl's filesystem. Defaults to
the [option -certfile], so one PEM file can hold both.

[para]

An encrypted key needs [option -password]. PKCS#8 keys encrypted with
PBES2, as written by [syscmd "openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -v2 aes-256-cbc"], work
with every backend, in PEM and DER form. Other formats depend on the
backend, see [sectref "BACKEND DIFFERENCES"].

[opt_def -model [arg channel]]
Use a shared configuration: certificates, keys, password, trust,
protocols, cipher suites, ALPN, [option -request] and [option -require]
come from it, and options of this call that configure them are ignored.
[option -servername], [option -command] and [option -validatecommand]
still apply. [arg channel] can be any channel. A TLS channel lends its own
configuration. Any other channel anchors one: the first import naming it
creates the configuration from its options, later imports share it, and it
is released when [arg channel] is closed; channels using it keep it.
[option -server] must match the configuration, or [cmd import] raises
[const "-model "][arg channel][const " is configured as a client"] (or
[const "server"]), errorCode [const "TLS MODEL"]. Sharing a configuration
saves loading files again and is needed for session resumption, see
[sectref "SESSION RESUMPTION"].

[opt_def -password [arg script]]
A script whose result is the password of the private key; the script may
also end with [cmd "return"] [arg password]. It is evaluated at global
level, once, by the [cmd import] that creates a configuration with a key,
whether or not the key is encrypted. An error in the script is raised by
[cmd import].

[opt_def -request [arg bool]]
On a server, ask the client for a certificate. Default [const 0]. A
certificate the client sends is then available through [cmd status]
whether or not it verifies, unless [option -require] is set. No effect on a
client.

[opt_def -require [arg bool]]
On a client, verify the server's certificate chain and host name and fail
the handshake if they do not verify. Default [const 1]. With [const 0], no
verification is done, unless [option -validatecommand] is given.
[para]
On a server, require a client certificate that verifies against
[option -cafile], [option -cadir] or the system store; implies
[option -request]. Default [const 0].

[opt_def -server [arg bool]]
Act as the server side. Default [const 0]. A server needs a certificate and
a private key, or [cmd import] raises an error.

[opt_def -servername [arg name]]
On a client, the name sent in the SNI extension and the name the server's
certificate must match. An IP address is matched against the certificate's
IP address entries and is not sent as SNI. Without [option -servername] no
SNI is sent and only the chain is verified, not the name; unlike
[cmd socket], [cmd import] does not set it. A value that is neither a host name
nor an IP address raises [const "invalid server name \""][arg name][const \"]
(errorCode [const "TLS CONFIG"]). Servers ignore this option.

[opt_def -ssl2 [arg bool]]
[opt_def -ssl3 [arg bool]]
[opt_def -tls1 [arg bool]]
[opt_def -tls1.1 [arg bool]]
[opt_def -castore [arg uri]]
[opt_def -dhparams [arg file]]
[opt_def -post_handshake [arg bool]]
[opt_def -security_level [arg n]]
[opt_def -session_id [arg data]]
Accepted for tcltls compatibility and ignored; the old protocols are never
enabled.

[opt_def -tls1.2 [arg bool]]
[opt_def -tls1.3 [arg bool]]
Enable TLS 1.2 or TLS 1.3. Both default to [const 1]. Disabling both
raises [const "no protocols enabled"] (errorCode [const "TLS PROTOCOL"]).

[opt_def -validatecommand [arg prefix]]
[opt_def -vcmd [arg prefix]]
A command prefix that decides on each certificate the peer sends, see
[sectref CALLBACKS]. A channel with this callback never resumes a session.

[list_end]

[section CALLBACKS]

[option -command] and [option -validatecommand] are command prefixes: the
arguments below are appended and the command is evaluated at global level.
They can run inside any command that does I/O on the channel, or from the
event loop when the channel is nonblocking. An error in a callback is
reported through [cmd bgerror] (see [cmd "interp bgerror"]); it does not
become the result of the command that triggered the callback.

[list_begin definitions]

[def "[arg prefix] [const info] [arg channel] [const handshake] [const done] [const "{SSL negotiation finished successfully}"] [const info]"]
[option -command], once, when the handshake has completed.

[def "[arg prefix] [const error] [arg channel] [arg message]"]
[option -command], once, when the channel fails, during the handshake or
later. [arg message] is the error that I/O on the channel raises from then
on.

[def "[arg prefix] [const verify] [arg channel] [arg depth] [arg cert] [arg ok] [arg error]"]
[option -validatecommand], during the handshake, for each certificate the
peer sent, from the highest [arg depth] down to [const 0], the peer's own
certificate. Certificates taken from the local trust store are not
reported. [arg cert] is a dictionary with the certificate keys of
[cmd status]. [arg ok] is [const 1] if the library accepts this
certificate and [const 0] if not; [arg error] then gives the reason, such
as [const "certificate has expired"], [const "hostname mismatch"] or
[const "issuer certificate not trusted"] for a certificate signed by a
rejected one, and is empty otherwise.

[para]

A result that is a true boolean or a nonzero integer accepts the
certificate. Anything else, or an error, rejects it, and the handshake
fails with [const "handshake failed: certificate rejected by validation callback"].
The result replaces the library's verdict whatever [option -require] says:
the callback can accept an expired or untrusted certificate or a name
mismatch, and can reject a valid one. A client calls it whenever it is
given; a server only with [option -request] or [option -require], when
the client sends a certificate.

[para]

The callback may use its channel, but the handshake is still in progress:
[cmd handshake] returns [const 0], reads return nothing, and what it
writes is sent once the handshake has completed.

[list_end]

The [option -password] script is not a prefix: it is evaluated as given,
without arguments, see [sectref OPTIONS].

[section "CHANNEL BEHAVIOUR"]

[list_begin itemized]

[item]
The TLS channel keeps the name of the channel below. It adds no channel
options of its own: [cmd "chan configure"] options other than the generic
ones, such as [option -peername], [option -sockname] or
[option -keepalive], are those of the channel below.

[item]
The handshake starts on first use. On a blocking channel the first
[cmd read], [cmd gets], [cmd flush] or [cmd tlschan::handshake] performs the
whole handshake and waits for it. On a nonblocking channel the handshake
proceeds from the event loop: at once if the channel was already
nonblocking when imported, otherwise after the first read or write attempt
or once a [cmd "chan event"] handler is set. [cmd tlschan::handshake] also
advances it. Data written before it completes is buffered and sent
afterwards. Readable and writable handlers are not called before the
handshake has completed or failed.

[item]
A failure is final: every later [cmd read], [cmd gets], [cmd puts] or
[cmd flush] raises the error, without errorCode, and a nonblocking channel
becomes readable and writable to report it. During the handshake the
message is [const "handshake failed: "][arg reason]; afterwards
[const "read failed: "][arg reason], [const "write failed: "][arg reason],
[const "connection closed"] or an operating system message such as
[const "connection reset by peer"]. Either way it can also be
[const "peer sent alert: "][arg description]. Typical handshake reasons are
[const "certificate verify failed: "][arg why],
[const "certificate rejected by validation callback"],
[const "peer did not return a certificate"] and
[const "connection closed during handshake"]; [arg why] is one of
[const "certificate has expired"], [const "certificate is not yet valid"],
[const "hostname mismatch"], [const "self-signed certificate"],
[const "unable to get local issuer certificate"] or
[const "issuer certificate not trusted"], or else the library's own words.

[item]
In TLS 1.3 a client's handshake completes before the server has checked
the client certificate. A rejection reaches the client as an alert on its
next read, as in [const "peer sent alert: certificate required"].

[item]
The end of the stream is reported as EOF both when the peer sent a
close_notify alert and when the connection was closed without one.

[item]
[cmd close] sends a close_notify alert if the handshake has completed,
writes out pending data, blocking if needed, and closes the channel below.
Tcl refuses to half-close a stacked channel: [cmd close] [arg channel]
[const write] raises
[const "half-close not applicable to stack of transformations"].

[item]
[cmd fcopy] and [cmd "chan copy"] work in both directions.

[list_end]

[section "SESSION RESUMPTION"]

All backends resume sessions, in TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3, by the same rules:

[list_begin itemized]

[item]
A server issues session tickets under a key that belongs to its
configuration, so it resumes only sessions of connections that shared that
configuration. The connections accepted by one [cmd "socket -server"]
share one.

[item]
A client keeps the most recent session for each server name, for up to 32
names, in its configuration, and offers it on the next connection to the
same [option -servername] that uses this configuration. A client therefore
resumes only when its connections share a configuration through
[option -model], for example one anchored on a long-lived channel:

[example {
set model [file tempfile]
proc https {args} {
    tlschan::socket -model $::model {*}$args
}
http::register https 443 https
}]

[item]
There is no resumption for a client without [option -servername], nor for
a client or a server with [option -validatecommand], whose callback must
see every handshake.

[item]
[cmd connection] reports whether a session was resumed as
[const session_reused].

[list_end]

[section "BACKEND DIFFERENCES"]

The backends behave alike except for the following, observed with
wolfSSL 5.9.4, rustls 0.23.46, Mbed TLS 3.6.7 and OpenSSL 3.6.5.

[list_begin definitions]

[def "Cipher suites"]
[package tlschan::wolfssl] and [package tlschan::rustls] have 3 TLS 1.3
suites and 6 TLS 1.2 suites, all ECDHE with AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305.
[package tlschan::mbedtls] and [package tlschan::openssl] add the CCM suites
to TLS 1.3 and offer many more TLS 1.2 suites (103 and 59 in the builds
above), including CBC, ARIA, Camellia, DHE and RSA key exchange; their lists
depend on how the library was built.

[def "ALPN choice"]
[package tlschan::wolfssl] chooses the first of the client's protocols that
the server also lists; the others choose the first of the server's protocols
that the client offers.

[def [option -cadir]]
[package tlschan::wolfssl] and [package tlschan::mbedtls] load every
certificate file in the directory. [package tlschan::rustls] loads files
named [file *.pem], [file *.crt] or like an OpenSSL hash link
([file 1a2b3c4d.0]). [package tlschan::openssl] needs an OpenSSL hashed
directory (see [syscmd "openssl rehash"]) and finds only certificates under
their hash names.

[def "Trust anchors"]
[package tlschan::openssl] also accepts a certificate that is not a CA,
given in [option -cafile], as a trust anchor; the others do not.

[def "System trust store"]
[package tlschan::wolfssl] uses the Windows or macOS store, elsewhere the
certificates in [file /etc/ssl/certs],
[file /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors] and [file /etc/pki/tls/certs], or if
none exists the bundle [file /etc/ssl/cert.pem] (on Haiku
[file /boot/system/data/ssl/CARootCertificates.pem]); it ignores
[var SSL_CERT_FILE] and [var SSL_CERT_DIR]. [package tlschan::rustls] uses
rustls-native-certs, which honours [var SSL_CERT_FILE] and
[var SSL_CERT_DIR], and falls back to Mozilla's root list when the system
has none. [package tlschan::mbedtls] reads [var SSL_CERT_FILE] and
[var SSL_CERT_DIR] if set, else the first CA bundle found at the usual
places, else [file /etc/ssl/certs]; on Windows it uses the Windows
certificate store, on macOS the keychain's trust settings.
[package tlschan::openssl] uses OpenSSL's default locations, which honour
[var SSL_CERT_FILE] and [var SSL_CERT_DIR], then the usual bundle files, and
on Windows with OpenSSL 3.2 or later the Windows store.

[def "Encrypted keys"]
Legacy encrypted PEM keys ([const "Proc-Type: 4,ENCRYPTED"]) and PKCS#8 keys
encrypted with PBES1, such as PBE-SHA1-3DES, work only with
[package tlschan::mbedtls] and [package tlschan::openssl];
[package tlschan::wolfssl] reports [const "unsupported key encryption"] for
them.

[def "Refused client certificates"]
The alert a server sends when it refuses a client certificate is the
library's. For a missing certificate it is [const "certificate required"] in
TLS 1.3; in TLS 1.2 [package tlschan::wolfssl] and
[package tlschan::openssl] send [const "handshake failure"],
[package tlschan::rustls] [const "certificate required"], and
[package tlschan::mbedtls] closes the connection without an alert, so the
client reports [const "connection closed during handshake"]. For an
untrusted certificate it is [const "unknown CA"], from
[package tlschan::rustls] [const "certificate unknown"]. A
[package tlschan::mbedtls] server in TLS 1.3 encrypts the alert with keys
the client no longer reads with, so the client's next read fails with a MAC
error instead (an Mbed TLS bug); the server still refuses.

[def "Received alerts"]
[package tlschan::mbedtls] reports a received alert as
[const "peer sent alert"] without its name: Mbed TLS 3.6 doesn't say which
one arrived.

[list_end]

[section COMPATIBILITY]

[subsection tcltls]

Code written for tcltls 2 mostly runs unchanged through the
[cmd ::tls] aliases of [package tlschan], or with [namespace ::tls] replaced
by [namespace ::tlschan] or a backend's namespace. The differences:

[list_begin itemized]

[item]
Not provided: [cmd tls::shutdown], [cmd tls::misc], [cmd tls::build-info],
the sample callbacks [cmd tls::callback], [cmd tls::password] and
[cmd tls::validate_command], and [var tls::debug]. Added:
[cmd exporter], [cmd randombytes], [cmd aesgcm-encrypt],
[cmd aesgcm-decrypt] and [cmd pbkdf2].

[item]
[cmd status]: [const cipher] is an IANA name,
also for TLS 1.2, where tcltls uses OpenSSL names; digests are upper case
hex; [const bits] is the key size, not its security strength. tcltls keys
not listed under [cmd status], such as [const peername],
[const verifyResult], [const keyUsage] or [const all], are absent;
[const self_signed], [const ca], [const num_extensions] and [const ocsp]
correspond to tcltls's [const selfSigned], [const basicConstraintsCA],
[const extCount] and [const ocspResponders].

[item]
[cmd connection]: [const state] is [const handshake], [const open] or
[const failed], not an OpenSSL state string, and only the keys listed
there exist.

[item]
[cmd ciphers] returns IANA names and ignores [arg verbose] and
[arg supported].

[item]
[option -command] reports only [const info] (handshake completion) and
[const error]; there are no [const message] and [const session] calls.

[item]
[option -validatecommand] is called only with [const verify], not with
[const alpn], [const hello] or [const sni]. Any true result accepts; tcltls
treats [const 2] as rejecting without aborting. Certificates from the local
trust store are not reported.

[item]
[option -password] is evaluated without arguments; tcltls appends
[const password], the read/write flag and the maximum length.

[item]
[option -cert] and [option -key] take PEM as well as DER;
[option -cafile], [option -certfile] and [option -keyfile] are read through
Tcl's filesystem.

[item]
Options without an effect here are accepted and ignored, see
[sectref OPTIONS].

[item]
errorCodes begin with [const TLS], see [sectref ERRORS].

[list_end]

[subsection tclmtls]

tclmtls is the package [package mtls] with commands in [namespace ::mtls];
its names do not clash with these packages. Compared with tclmtls:

[list_begin itemized]

[item]
There is nothing like [cmd mtls::debug] or [cmd mtls::build-info].

[item]
[cmd tlschan::pkgconfig] has only the keys [const version] and
[const backend]; tclmtls's [const client], [const server],
[arg proto][const ,protocol] and [const version,backend],
[const name,backend], [const type,backend] are absent.

[item]
[cmd status] returns more keys, and cipher suites, there and in
[cmd ciphers], are IANA names ([const TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384]) instead of
Mbed TLS names ([const TLS1-3-AES-256-GCM-SHA384]).

[item]
errorCodes begin with [const TLS] instead of [const MTLS], and errors of the
crypto commands are just [const "TLS CRYPTO"], without a further element.
The crypto commands take and return data in the same formats and with the
same limits.

[list_end]

[section ERRORS]

Errors raised by the commands have these errorCodes:

[list_begin definitions]
[def "[const "TLS CONFIG"]"]
An invalid option value: unparsable certificate, key or CA file, wrong
password, unknown cipher suite, invalid ALPN name or server name, missing
certificate on a server.
[def "[const "TLS PROTOCOL"]"]
No protocol enabled.
[def "[const "TLS MODEL"]"]
The [option -model] configuration is for the other side, client or server.
[def "[const "TLS IMPORT"]"]
The session could not be set up.
[def "[const "TLS CHANNEL"]"]
The channel is not a TLS channel.
[def "[const "TLS HANDSHAKE"]"]
Raised by [cmd handshake] for a failed handshake.
[def "[const "TLS WRONGSTATE"] [const "TLS WRONGARGS"] [const "TLS EXPORT"]"]
Raised by [cmd exporter].
[def "[const "TLS CRYPTO"]"]
Raised by the crypto commands.
[def "[const "TLS INIT"]"]
The library could not be initialised when loading the package.
[def "[const "TLS BACKEND"]"]
[package tlschan] found no backend to load.
[list_end]

Errors opening a file named in an option have a [const POSIX] errorCode;
errors in arguments have the usual [const TCL] ones. I/O errors on the
channel do not set errorCode, see [sectref "CHANNEL BEHAVIOUR"].

[section "STATIC LINKING"]

Built with [const BUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF], each backend is an archive, such
as [file libtcl9tlschan_wolfssl0.1.0.a], whose only global symbol is its
initialisation function: [fun Tlschan_wolfssl_Init],
[fun Tlschan_rustls_Init], [fun Tlschan_mbedtls_Init] or
[fun Tlschan_openssl_Init]. The archive uses Tcl's stubs interface, so the
application also links Tcl's stub library; the pkg-config file
[file tcl9tlschan_wolfssl.pc] (and so on) names the other libraries to
link. The application registers the package and declares it in each
interpreter that is to use it:

[example {
Tcl_StaticLibrary(NULL, "Tlschan_wolfssl", Tlschan_wolfssl_Init, NULL);
Tcl_EvalEx(interp,
    "package ifneeded tlschan::wolfssl 0.1.0 {load {} Tlschan_wolfssl}", -1, 0);
}]

Pass NULL, not an interpreter: with one, Tcl takes the package as already
loaded there and never calls [fun Tlschan_wolfssl_Init]. There is no
safe-interpreter initialisation function.

[para]

[package tlschan] is a script, installed with its [file pkgIndex.tcl] in
[file lib/tlschan0.1.0] for static builds too. An application ships it in
its script library, next to the backends it registers, and
[cmd "package require tlschan"] then picks among those. See
[file README.md] for building.

[section EXAMPLES]

Fetch a page over HTTPS, verifying the server against the system store:

[example {
package require tlschan
package require http
http::register https 443 ::tlschan::socket
set tok [http::geturl https://www.tcl-lang.org/]
puts [http::ncode $tok]
http::cleanup $tok
}]

An echo server, event driven:

[example {
package require tlschan

proc accept {chan addr port} {
    chan configure $chan -blocking 0 -buffering line
    chan event $chan readable [list echo $chan]
}

proc echo {chan} {
    if {[catch {gets $chan line} n]} {
        puts stderr "$chan: $n"
        close $chan
    } elseif {$n >= 0} {
        puts $chan $line
    } elseif {[eof $chan]} {
        close $chan
    }
}

set opts {-certfile server.pem -keyfile server-key.pem}
tlschan::socket -server accept {*}$opts 8443
vwait forever
}]

Switch an established plain connection to TLS, as after a STARTTLS
command; [option -servername] must be given explicitly:

[example {
package require tlschan
set chan [socket mail.example.com 587]
# ... plain text exchange ending in STARTTLS ...
tlschan::starttls $chan -servername mail.example.com
tlschan::handshake $chan
}]

Accept a server only if its certificate has a known SHA-256 fingerprint,
such as one shown by [syscmd "openssl x509 -noout -fingerprint -sha256"].
This one pins the backend by requiring it directly:

[example {
package require tlschan::mbedtls

proc pin {fingerprint op chan depth cert ok error} {
    if {$depth > 0} {
        return 1
    }
    set expected [string map {: ""} $fingerprint]
    string equal -nocase $expected [dict get $cert sha256_hash]
}

set vcmd [list pin $fingerprint]
set chan [tlschan::mbedtls::socket -validatecommand $vcmd device.local 8443]
}]

Mutual TLS: the server requires a client certificate issued by its CA and
greets the client by name.

[example {
package require tlschan

proc accept {chan addr port} {
    chan configure $chan -blocking 0 -buffering line
    chan event $chan readable [list greet $chan]
}

proc greet {chan} {
    if {[catch {gets $chan line} n] || [eof $chan]} {
        close $chan
    } elseif {$n >= 0} {
        puts $chan "hello [dict get [tlschan::status $chan] subject]"
    }
}

set opts {-certfile server.pem -keyfile server-key.pem}
tlschan::socket -server accept -require 1 -cafile client-ca.pem {*}$opts 8443
vwait forever
}]

The client:

[example {
package require tlschan
set opts {-certfile client.pem -keyfile client-key.pem}
set chan [tlschan::socket -cafile server-ca.pem {*}$opts server.example.com 8443]
chan configure $chan -buffering line
puts $chan hi
puts [gets $chan]
}]

Code written for tcltls, unchanged:

[example {
package require tlschan
set chan [tls::socket www.tcl-lang.org 443]
}]

[see_also socket chan http]
[manpage_end]
